        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.218,
            0.624,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": "\\*16. Suppose you drop a dye at random on the rectangular region shown in Fig. 7.8. What is the probability that it will land inside the circle with a diameter of 1 m?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.635,
            0.17,
            0.879,
            0.288
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.721,
                0.294,
                0.79,
                0.312
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.8"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.37,
            0.322,
            0.63,
            0.344
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Chapter Summary"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.359,
            0.806,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "z Probability is a measurement of the likelihood of an event."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.383,
            0.886,
            0.461
          ],
          "content": "Probability is expressed on a scale from 0 (indicating impossibility) to 1 (indicating certainty), representing the likelihood of an event occurring. The probability of an event E is denoted as P(E), where $0 \\leq \\mathbb { P } ( \\mathbb { E } ) \\leq 1$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.465,
            0.884,
            0.505
          ],
          "content": "Experimental probability is determined by conducting an experiment and is calculated as:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.508,
            0.874,
            0.557
          ],
          "content": "Number of times the event occurred Experimental Probability Total number of trials"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.56,
            0.884,
            0.602
          ],
          "content": "Theoretical probability is based on all possible outcomes in a fair situation, assuming each is equally likely:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.603,
            0.599,
            0.624
          ],
          "content": "Theoretical Probability of an event A is"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.628,
            0.606,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{A})=\\frac{\\text { Number of failures } \\text { outcomes }}{\\text { Number of possible outcomes }}",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.683,
            0.884,
            0.743
          ],
          "content": "z A sample space is the list of all possible outcomes of a random experiment. It is represented as a set $\\mathrm{S} = \\mathrm{function}_{1},$ outcome …, outcome }."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.746,
            0.886,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "An event is any one or a group of possible outcomes from a random experiment. It is like picking results or outcomes from all that could happen."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.807,
            0.887,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": "Tree diagrams help us to list and visualise all possible outcomes of a random experiment. They also help us calculate probabilities of events related to random experiments."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
